00:34:04,320 --> 00:34:06,320
At the end of that book,

825
00:34:06,320 --> 00:34:08,319
there's a chapter on

826
00:34:08,319 --> 00:34:12,319
the seven most important ideas in finance.

827
00:34:12,319 --> 00:34:15,319
And one of the ideas is efficient markets.

828
00:34:15,319 --> 00:34:17,319
And quoting the textbook,

829
00:34:17,320 --> 00:34:19,320
Brealey and Myers say,

830
00:34:19,320 --> 00:34:23,320
security prices accurately reflect available information

831
00:34:23,320 --> 00:34:26,320
and respond rapidly to new information

832
00:34:26,320 --> 00:34:28,320
as soon as it becomes available.

833
00:34:28,320 --> 00:34:29,320
Okay.

834
00:34:29,320 --> 00:34:32,320
They do qualify it in 1984.

835
00:34:32,320 --> 00:34:35,320
Don't misunderstand the efficient markets idea.

836
00:34:35,320 --> 00:34:38,320
It doesn't say there are no taxes or costs.

837
00:34:38,320 --> 00:34:40,320
It doesn't say there aren't some clever people

838
00:34:40,320 --> 00:34:42,320
and some stupid ones.

839
00:34:42,320 --> 00:34:44,320
It merely implies that competition

840
00:34:44,320 --> 00:34:46,320
in actual capital markets

841
00:34:46,320 --> 00:34:48,320
is very tough.

842
00:34:48,320 --> 00:34:50,320
There are no money machines

843
00:34:50,320 --> 00:34:52,320
and security prices reflect

844
00:34:52,320 --> 00:34:55,320
the true underlying value of assets.

845
00:34:55,320 --> 00:34:57,320
Okay. Let me repeat that.

846
00:34:57,320 --> 00:34:59,320
Security prices reflect

847
00:34:59,320 --> 00:35:01,320
the true underlying value of assets.

848
00:35:01,320 --> 00:35:03,320
Okay.

849
00:35:03,320 --> 00:35:05,320
That's almost,

850
00:35:05,320 --> 00:35:07,320
that's a pretty strong statement, right?

851
00:35:07,320 --> 00:35:09,320
But that's efficient market.

852
00:35:09,320 --> 00:35:10,820
They just said it in

853
00:35:10,820 --> 00:35:12,320
their second edition of the book.

854
00:35:12,320 --> 00:35:15,320
Not many people would say that, right?

855
00:35:15,320 --> 00:35:16,320
Trust the stock market.

856
00:35:16,320 --> 00:35:19,320
Don't trust the people you know and love and trust.

857
00:35:19,320 --> 00:35:21,320
Trust the stock market.

858
00:35:21,320 --> 00:35:24,320
Well, they deleted that from later editions of their book.

859
00:35:24,320 --> 00:35:29,320
And I think that's a sign of changes.

860
00:35:29,320 --> 00:35:32,320
So, I was looking at the 2008 edition.

861
00:35:32,320 --> 00:35:34,320
They've now taken on a third author.

862
00:35:34,320 --> 00:35:35,820
They're getting tired of coming out with

863
00:35:35,820 --> 00:35:37,320
more and more editions of their book.

864
00:35:37,320 --> 00:35:41,320
So, they've taken on Franklin Allen from the Wharton School.

865
00:35:41,320 --> 00:35:43,320
They've deleted what I just read.

866
00:35:43,320 --> 00:35:44,320
Now it says,

867
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:46,320
I'm quoting from them,

868
00:35:46,320 --> 00:35:48,320
much more research is needed

869
00:35:48,320 --> 00:35:50,320
before we have a full understanding

870
00:35:50,320 --> 00:35:52,320
of why asset prices

871
00:35:52,320 --> 00:35:55,320
sometimes get so out of line

872
00:35:55,320 --> 00:35:58,320
with what appears to be their discounted future payoffs.

873
00:35:58,320 --> 00:36:01,320
All right. That's a complete turnaround in the textbook.

874
00:36:01,320 --> 00:36:04,320
So, this is one of the most popular textbooks.

875
00:36:04,320 --> 00:36:06,320
And they've changed completely.

876
00:36:06,320 --> 00:36:09,320
So, I think we have an idea

877
00:36:09,320 --> 00:36:13,320
that started in 19, around the 1960s.

878
00:36:13,320 --> 00:36:14,320
It was somehow

879
00:36:14,320 --> 00:36:17,320
associated with computers and electronic databases

880
00:36:17,320 --> 00:36:21,320
and modern thinking and mathematical finance.

881
00:36:21,320 --> 00:36:23,320
And they kind of went too far with it.

882
00:36:23,320 --> 00:36:27,320
And they concluded that you just can't beat the market.

883
00:36:27,320 --> 00:36:29,320
Another thing I put on the reading list

884
00:36:29,320 --> 00:36:32,320
is a reading from the New Yorker magazine.

885
00:36:32,320 --> 00:36:36,320
It just came out in December.

886
00:36:36,320 --> 00:36:38,320
I thought it was relevant.

887
00:36:38,320 --> 00:36:40,320
It's by Jonah Lehrer.

888
00:36:40,320 --> 00:36:42,320
And the title of the article is

889
00:36:42,320 --> 00:36:44,320
The Truth Wears Off.

890
00:36:44,320 --> 00:36:46,320
Subtitle,

891
00:36:46,320 --> 00:36:50,320
Is There Something Wrong With The Scientific Method?

892
00:36:50,320 --> 00:36:52,320
Well, I don't know if there's anything wrong

893
00:36:52,320 --> 00:36:54,320
with the scientific method,

894
00:36:54,320 --> 00:36:56,320
but I was interested in this article.

895
00:36:56,320 --> 00:36:59,320
Because what the New Yorker article points out

896
00:36:59,320 --> 00:37:02,320
is that a lot of scientists,

897
00:37:02,320 --> 00:37:04,320
and this is outside of finance,

898
00:37:04,320 --> 00:37:06,320
I'm making a parallel here.

899
00:37:06,320 --> 00:37:10,320
A lot of scientists who follow careful scientific procedures

900
00:37:10,320 --> 00:37:12,320
seem to generate results

901
00:37:12,320 --> 00:37:14,320
that are later discredited.

902
00:37:14,320 --> 00:37:17,320
And nobody can figure out why.

903
00:37:17,320 --> 00:37:20,320
It's like the universe is changing.

904
00:37:20,320 --> 00:37:23,320
He gives an example in the New Yorker article.

905
00:37:23,320 --> 00:37:26,320
And this is from drugs.

906
00:37:26,320 --> 00:37:28,320
There's a drug, which are,

907
00:37:28,320 --> 00:37:30,320
a class of drugs called

908
00:37:30,320 --> 00:37:32,320
second generation antipsychotics.

909
00:37:32,320 --> 00:37:35,320
These are used for people who are either

910
00:37:35,320 --> 00:37:37,320
schizophrenic or,

911
00:37:37,320 --> 00:37:40,320
I guess it can be used more generally than that.

912
00:37:40,320 --> 00:37:42,320
Some of the drugs are called

913
00:37:42,320 --> 00:37:44,320
antipsyroquil, zyprexa.

914
00:37:44,320 --> 00:37:47,320
When these drugs were first introduced,

915
00:37:47,320 --> 00:37:50,320
careful studies that passed muster

916
00:37:50,320 --> 00:37:52,320
in the best medical journals

917
00:37:52,320 --> 00:37:54,320
found that they were highly effective.

918
00:37:54,320 --> 00:37:57,320
And they were written up as a godsend.

919
00:37:57,320 --> 00:38:00,320
A way of dealing with problems that

920
00:38:00,320 --> 00:38:02,320
used to weigh on people and,

921
00:38:02,320 --> 00:38:04,320
wonderful, okay.

922
00:38:04,320 --> 00:38:07,320
The medical procedures involve

923
00:38:07,320 --> 00:38:10,320
careful controls on studies, including

924
00:38:10,320 --> 00:38:12,320
a double-blind procedure.

925
00:38:12,320 --> 00:38:14,320
And a double-blind procedure,

926
00:38:14,320 --> 00:38:16,320
when you want to test a drug on human subjects,

927
00:38:16,320 --> 00:38:20,320
you have to have both the subject doesn't know

928
00:38:20,320 --> 00:38:22,320
whether he or she is getting the drug,

929
00:38:22,320 --> 00:38:25,320
and the experimenter who runs the experiment

930
00:38:25,320 --> 00:38:27,320
doesn't know which one is the drug.

931
00:38:27,320 --> 00:38:30,320
So, you give bottle A and bottle B

932
00:38:30,320 --> 00:38:31,320
to the experimenter.

933
00:38:31,320 --> 00:38:33,320
And the experimenter is never told

934
00:38:33,320 --> 00:38:35,320
which one is zyprexa

935
00:38:35,320 --> 00:38:37,320
and which one is a placebo.

936
00:38:37,320 --> 00:38:39,320
And then the experimenter has to write up

937
00:38:39,320 --> 00:38:41,320
a whole report on drug A and drug B.

938
00:38:41,320 --> 00:38:42,320
Not even the drug A.

939
00:38:42,320 --> 00:38:44,320
Not even knowing.

940
00:38:44,320 --> 00:38:45,320
Okay?

941
00:38:45,320 --> 00:38:47,320
This is to eliminate any possible bias.

942
00:38:47,320 --> 00:38:49,320
So, these drugs passed that.

943
00:38:49,320 --> 00:38:50,320
You see what I'm saying?

944
00:38:50,320 --> 00:38:51,320
The controls were right.

945
00:38:51,320 --> 00:38:53,320
Everything was good.

946
00:38:53,320 --> 00:38:55,320
But then, as years go by,

947
00:38:55,320 --> 00:38:57,320
the tests start coming out,

948
00:38:57,320 --> 00:38:59,320
the new attempts to back up,

949
00:38:59,320 --> 00:39:01,320
replicate those, start coming out

950
00:39:01,320 --> 00:39:02,320
more negative.

951
00:39:02,320 --> 00:39:04,320
They didn't disprove the drugs.

952
00:39:04,320 --> 00:39:07,320
They just weren't such wonder drugs

953
00:39:07,320 --> 00:39:08,320
as they thought.

954
00:39:08,320 --> 00:39:10,320
So, how can that be?

955
00:39:10,320 --> 00:39:11,320
And what the article says,

956
00:39:11,320 --> 00:39:14,320
well, it must be that somehow scientific bias,

957
00:39:14,320 --> 00:39:17,320
when there's an enthusiasm for some new theory,

958
00:39:17,320 --> 00:39:18,320
it creeps in,

959
00:39:18,320 --> 00:39:21,320
even if you try to make the strongest controls.

960
00:39:21,320 --> 00:39:22,320
And you say, how could that happen

961
00:39:22,320 --> 00:39:24,320
with a double-blind procedure?

962
00:39:24,320 --> 00:39:27,320
Well, maybe they broke the double-blind somehow.

963
00:39:27,320 --> 00:39:28,320
They tried,

964
00:39:28,320 --> 00:39:30,320
but the guy, experimenter, figured out.

965
00:39:30,320 --> 00:39:32,320
And then he started,

966
00:39:32,320 --> 00:39:34,320
not deliberately fabricating results,

967
00:39:34,320 --> 00:39:36,320
but it's the kind of thing where

968
00:39:36,320 --> 00:39:38,320
one subject says,

969
00:39:38,320 --> 00:39:40,320
I didn't take my Aplafi regularly.

970
00:39:40,320 --> 00:39:42,320
I took two tablets last week.

971
00:39:42,320 --> 00:39:44,320
I have to decide whether to throw this person

972
00:39:44,320 --> 00:39:46,320
out of the sample.

973
00:39:46,320 --> 00:39:48,320
And then I kind of remember that it wasn't,

974
00:39:48,320 --> 00:39:50,320
the drug wasn't working for this person.

975
00:39:50,320 --> 00:39:52,320
And it colors my judgment,

976
00:39:52,320 --> 00:39:54,320
so I throw them out.

977
00:39:54,320 --> 00:39:56,320
And another thing that happens is that

978
00:39:56,320 --> 00:39:58,320
the studies that didn't find it

979
00:39:58,320 --> 00:40:00,320
might have been suppressed.

980
00:40:00,320 --> 00:40:02,320
Someone might have done an Aplafi test

981
00:40:02,320 --> 00:40:04,320
and gotten bad results.

982
00:40:04,320 --> 00:40:06,320
And then showed it to their superior and said,

983
00:40:06,320 --> 00:40:07,320
should I publish this?

984
00:40:07,320 --> 00:40:08,320
And the superior said,

985
00:40:08,320 --> 00:40:09,320
wait a minute,

986
00:40:09,320 --> 00:40:11,320
there must be something wrong here.

987
00:40:11,320 --> 00:40:13,320
Aplafi is wonderful.

988
00:40:13,320 --> 00:40:14,320
So, let's look.

989
00:40:14,320 --> 00:40:16,320
And then they find something that might be wrong.

990
00:40:16,320 --> 00:40:18,320
And he said, you shouldn't publish this,

991
00:40:18,320 --> 00:40:20,320
because they find something.

992
00:40:20,320 --> 00:40:23,320
So, the publication process is biased

993
00:40:23,320 --> 00:40:25,320
for a while.

994
00:40:25,320 --> 00:40:27,320
But eventually it catches up.

995
00:40:27,320 --> 00:40:29,320
So, I think the same thing happened

996
00:40:29,320 --> 00:40:31,320
with the efficient markets hypothesis.

997
00:40:31,320 --> 00:40:33,320
In the initial enthusiasm,

998
00:40:33,320 --> 00:40:36,320
anybody who found that the efficient markets hypothesis

999
00:40:36,320 --> 00:40:38,320
wasn't supported by the evidence,

1000
00:40:38,320 --> 00:40:40,320
that person would be told, look again,

1001
00:40:40,320 --> 00:40:42,320
maybe you've done something wrong.

1002
00:40:42,320 --> 00:40:48,320
And so, that's what happened.

1003
00:40:48,320 --> 00:40:56,320
Now, I wanted to do a little bit more history

1004
00:40:56,320 --> 00:41:01,320
and describe the concept of random walk,

1005
00:41:01,320 --> 00:41:05,320
which is central to the efficient markets hypothesis.

1006
00:41:05,320 --> 00:41:07,320
And,

1007
00:41:07,320 --> 00:41:13,320
let me start, though,

1008
00:41:13,320 --> 00:41:15,320
with a little bit more history.

1009
00:41:15,320 --> 00:41:17,320
Technical analysis.

1010
00:41:25,320 --> 00:41:27,320
This term goes back,

1011
00:41:27,320 --> 00:41:29,320
must be over 100 years.

1012
00:41:29,320 --> 00:41:33,320
Technical analysis is the analysis of stock prices,

1013
00:41:33,320 --> 00:41:36,320
or maybe other speculative asset prices,

1014
00:41:36,320 --> 00:41:37,320
by looking at the market.

1015
00:41:37,320 --> 00:41:40,320
By looking at charts of the prices.

1016
00:41:40,320 --> 00:41:48,320
And looking for patterns that suggest movements in prices.

1017
00:41:48,320 --> 00:41:51,320
The classic text of technical analysis

1018
00:41:51,320 --> 00:41:55,320
is Edwards and McGee.

1019
00:41:55,320 --> 00:41:57,320
McGee has a famous story about,

1020
00:41:57,320 --> 00:41:58,320
he was not a professor,

1021
00:41:58,320 --> 00:42:02,320
he was a Wall Street analyst.

1022
00:42:02,320 --> 00:42:04,320
And the story about him is that

1023
00:42:04,320 --> 00:42:06,320
he believed that you look at the prices

1024
00:42:06,320 --> 00:42:08,320
and you can predict prices.

1025
00:42:08,320 --> 00:42:09,320
And, in fact, he said,

1026
00:42:09,320 --> 00:42:10,320
I don't want to look at anything else.

1027
00:42:10,320 --> 00:42:12,320
I just want to see prices.

1028
00:42:12,320 --> 00:42:14,320
I'll do plots and I can,

1029
00:42:14,320 --> 00:42:16,320
using my judgment,

1030
00:42:16,320 --> 00:42:17,320
I can find,

1031
00:42:17,320 --> 00:42:19,320
I can figure out what it's going to do.

1032
00:42:19,320 --> 00:42:21,320
And so, the story about McGee is,

1033
00:42:21,320 --> 00:42:23,320
everyone on Wall Street wants the corner office,

1034
00:42:23,320 --> 00:42:28,320
overlooking the World Trade Center or whatever.

1035
00:42:28,320 --> 00:42:31,320
He said, I want an interior office with no windows.

1036
00:42:31,320 --> 00:42:33,320
I don't want any distractions.

1037
00:42:33,320 --> 00:42:35,320
I don't want the real world impinging on my judgment.

1038
00:42:35,320 --> 00:42:38,320
So, that's McGee.

1039
00:42:38,320 --> 00:42:40,320
But he said that there are certain things

1040
00:42:40,320 --> 00:42:42,320
that you see obviously.

1041
00:42:42,320 --> 00:42:45,320
For example, resistance level.

1042
00:42:49,320 --> 00:42:52,320
When the Dow Jones Industrial Average

1043
00:42:52,320 --> 00:42:54,320
approached 1,000,

1044
00:42:54,320 --> 00:42:56,320
I think it was in the 60s, 1960s.

1045
00:42:56,320 --> 00:42:59,320
It's way above that now, as you know.

1046
00:42:59,320 --> 00:43:02,320
But when it first approached 1,000,

1047
00:43:02,320 --> 00:43:04,320
technical analysts said,

1048
00:43:04,320 --> 00:43:07,320
you know, maybe it's going to have trouble crossing 1,000.

1049
00:43:07,320 --> 00:43:09,320
Because that's a psychological barrier.

1050
00:43:09,320 --> 00:43:11,320
That sounds magical.

1051
00:43:11,320 --> 00:43:13,320
How can the Dow be worth over 1,000?

1052
00:43:13,320 --> 00:43:15,320
Wow, I'm going to sell.

1053
00:43:15,320 --> 00:43:17,320
And so, the idea was that people would sell

1054
00:43:17,320 --> 00:43:19,320
when it approached 1,000.

1055
00:43:19,320 --> 00:43:21,320
And it seemed, the technical analysts seemed to be right,

1056
00:43:21,320 --> 00:43:24,320
because the Dow bounced around just below 1,000

1057
00:43:24,320 --> 00:43:25,320
for a long time.

1058
00:43:25,320 --> 00:43:27,320
I guess it was months or a year.

1059
00:43:27,320 --> 00:43:30,320
Like it couldn't cross the resistance level.

1060
00:43:30,320 --> 00:43:32,320
So, that's one example.

1061
00:43:32,320 --> 00:43:34,320
I have another example,

1062
00:43:34,320 --> 00:43:37,320
which is from Edwards and McGee's book.

1063
00:43:37,320 --> 00:43:40,320
How do I get this?

1064
00:43:47,320 --> 00:43:49,320
It's not coming up.

1065
00:43:49,320 --> 00:43:53,320
Oh, that's because my thing went into hibernate.

1066
00:43:56,320 --> 00:43:58,320
I have to do my password.

1067
00:44:01,320 --> 00:44:02,320
There we are.

1068
00:44:02,320 --> 00:44:04,320
That's Edwards and McGee.

1069
00:44:04,320 --> 00:44:06,320
This is one of the patterns.

1070
00:44:06,320 --> 00:44:10,320
McGee was actually a student of psychology.

1071
00:44:10,320 --> 00:44:12,320
And he thought,

1072
00:44:12,320 --> 00:44:14,820
certain kinds of patterns seem

1073
00:44:14,820 --> 00:44:17,320
to have really spooked people.

1074
00:44:17,320 --> 00:44:19,320
And this is one pattern,

1075
00:44:19,320 --> 00:44:21,320
which he called head and shoulders.

1076
00:44:21,320 --> 00:44:22,320
Okay?

1077
00:44:22,320 --> 00:44:24,320
That's the head.

1078
00:44:24,320 --> 00:44:25,320
That's one shoulder.

1079
00:44:25,320 --> 00:44:27,320
That's the other shoulder.

1080
00:44:27,320 --> 00:44:29,320
And he said, when you see this pattern,

1081
00:44:29,320 --> 00:44:30,320
watch out.

1082
00:44:30,320 --> 00:44:33,320
It's going to actually totally collapse,

1083
00:44:33,320 --> 00:44:35,320
as it is shown doing.

1084
00:44:35,320 --> 00:44:37,320
And so, he had you plotting.

1085
00:44:37,320 --> 00:44:40,320
These are stock prices plotted against time.

1086
00:44:40,320 --> 00:44:42,320
This is, I don't know what the, these are days.

1087
00:44:42,320 --> 00:44:45,320
Each of these points is a day.

1088
00:44:45,320 --> 00:44:47,320
And this is from their book.

1089
00:44:47,320 --> 00:44:49,320
So, it's a hypothetical.

1090
00:44:49,320 --> 00:44:51,820
You hardly ever see such

1091
00:44:51,820 --> 00:44:54,320
perfect head and shoulders patterns.

1092
00:44:54,320 --> 00:44:59,320
And so, that's one of the patterns.

1093
00:44:59,320 --> 00:45:01,320
Maybe that's his most famous.

1094
00:45:01,320 --> 00:45:02,320
Edwards and McGee most famous.

1095
00:45:02,320 --> 00:45:06,320
Head and shoulders.

1096
00:45:06,320 --> 00:45:08,320
So, the question is, does it work?

1097
00:45:08,320 --> 00:45:12,320
Does it really work?

1098
